# AGENTS.md — your onboarding

You are maintaining __CURVATURE_TITLE__, a Curvature app. This file is the
documentation; everything else is enforced, not remembered.

## The one command

```bash
./gate.sh        # ruff + pyright + pytest with coverage + curvature check
```

Green means done. Red means not done. `curvature check` findings are
called anomalies; each names the invariant it serves and the message
tells you the fix. If a finding seems wrong, stop and say so — never
route around the gate.

## Recipes

**Component**: `uv run curvature new component app/components/<name>` —
pours the file pair (component + test) already on-curve. Declare the
Props model first; the component is a pure function of it. Its CSS
lives beside it.

**Route**: reads render, writes redirect. No exceptions you can grant
yourself.

```python
@app.get("/things")
async def things(request: Request):
    return respond(
        request, thing_list(props), shell=shell,
        purpose="Review the current things",
    )

@app.post("/things")
async def create_thing(name: Annotated[str, Form()]):
    store.add(name.strip())
    return redirect("/things")
```

Every fragment root carries an id. Forms are real forms, links are real
links — the constructors refuse anything else.

## The four nevers

1. Never write application JavaScript outside a declared Event Horizon. The
   framework client layer owns ordinary navigation and Live lifecycle; normal
   behavior belongs on the server or in native HTML. Product behavior that
   genuinely cannot tolerate a round trip needs the operator's declaration
   first, then an exact `app/static/vendor/<name>/event-horizon.json` fence,
   byte and capability budgets, and consumer tests for its server contract and
   JavaScript-off baseline. The schema lives in Curvature's
   [Event Horizon reference](https://github.com/siliconsociety/Curvature/blob/main/docs/EVENT_HORIZONS.md).
2. Never edit ratchet.toml. `uv run curvature ratchet` is the only hand on
   the mechanism.
3. Never bypass the gate — no skipped tests, no weakened assertions.
4. Never add a template language, bundler, client state store, or
   plugin registry.

Spiral is active by default. Substantial direct neighbors let healthy leaves
grow by their local radius, while a directory branches beyond twelve
meaningful children and hollow branches are pruned. Explicit boundaries or
opt-out live in `pyproject.toml`; see the framework's `docs/SPIRAL.md`.

This repository is the agent control plane. AGENTS.md and `./gate.sh` are
sufficient for ordinary work; do not invoke a framework-specific agent skill
for each edit.

## Field reports

When app work reveals a gap owned by Curvature — the app is reimplementing
framework responsibility or compensating for a missing check — identify it to
the operator and offer to file an upstream Curvature issue. Filing requires the
operator's nod. That authorization permits the upstream issue; it creates no
issue, attribution policy, or process artifact in this consumer repository. Do
not silently ship the workaround. A good report names the consumer context and
mechanism, gives a minimal reproduction, includes measurements where relevant,
records the temporary workaround and verification performed, and states desired
behavior as checkable invariants.

## When you finish

Run ./gate.sh. If green and coverage rose, run `uv run curvature ratchet`
and include the tightened file in your change. Report what you built,
what the gate said, and every shortcut, doubt, or failed attempt that
shaped the result.
